description:
Pass 2 idiom finding under epic PKIX-qwzx.

Two related collection-wrapper idiom misses on `pkix-path-builder::CertPool`:

**1. `CertPool::new()` is not `const fn`.**

`pkix-path-builder/src/lib.rs:64-69`:
```rust
impl CertPool {
    /// Create an empty pool.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self::default()
    }
}
```

`Vec::new()` is `const`. Making `CertPool::new` `const fn` (as a direct
struct literal `Self { certs: Vec::new() }` rather than `Self::default()`)
allows callers to write `static EMPTY_POOL: CertPool = CertPool::new();`,
matching the pattern already used by `PathBuilderConfig::new` in the same file
(line 327: `pub const fn new() -> Self { ... }`).

**2. `CertPool` lacks `FromIterator<Certificate>` and `Extend<Certificate>` impls.**

The Rust idiom for a single-field collection wrapper is to provide:
- `FromIterator<T>` so callers can write `let pool: CertPool = certs.into_iter().collect()`
- `Extend<T>` so callers can write `pool.extend(more_certs)`

`CertPool` already has `IntoIterator` for `&CertPool` (line 101) but not the
inverse. This is asymmetric and forces callers into a manual loop:
```rust
let mut pool = CertPool::new();
for c in candidates { pool.add(c); }   // could be `pool.extend(candidates)`
```

Fix: add both impls; they are 5-line each. Same idiom as `Vec` and `HashMap`.

```rust
impl FromIterator<Certificate> for CertPool {
    fn from_iter<I: IntoIterator<Item = Certificate>>(iter: I) -> Self {
        Self { certs: iter.into_iter().collect() }
    }
}
impl Extend<Certificate> for CertPool {
    fn extend<I: IntoIterator<Item = Certificate>>(&mut self, iter: I) {
        self.certs.extend(iter);
    }
}
```

Locations: `pkix-path-builder/src/lib.rs:60-99`
